    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some complex dermatological claims and I'm seeing a high rate of denials for \"atypical\" presentations. Specifically, when we have lesions that present with irregular, jagged margins and unusual pigmentary distributions that don't fit standard subtypes, the payers are kicking them back for lack of specificity. Can you clarify the proper hierarchy for coding irregular skin morphology and how we should differentiate between benign morphological irregularities and those requiring more specific pathological coding under the L-series?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The ICD-10 coding system has various codes that accommodate atypical skin morphologies. The H-series and L-series codes are suggested for unusual presentations, with the F-series being listed last for highly abnormal findings. Typically, a provider should start by assigning an L-code for lesions with atypical morphology, then move to the corresponding F-code if necessary for more abnormal presentations. However, consulting with a dermatopathologist or physician colleague may provide more specific guidance, as denials often rely on payer company policies and in-house review.",
      "source": "target",
