    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, I think you might have transposed some of the series. I'm fairly certain the F-series covers mental and behavioral disorders, not dermatological pathology. I meant to focus on the distinction between the L-codes for skin diseases and the more specific diagnostic markers used for pigmentary irregularities. \n\nCould you go into more detail on how to handle cases where the morphology is just... off? Like when the border of a lesion is highly irregular or the texture is oddly pebbled or uneven in a way that doesn't immediately align with a standard nevus or carcinoma? I need to know how to document that specific irregularity to meet the highest level of specificity required for reimbursement.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct that the F-series is not typically used for dermatological coding. The L-series codes do indeed cover various skin diseases.\n\nTo handle cases with unusual morphology, you should consider using more specific L-series codes that describe the lesion's characteristics. For example, the \"L56.2 - Skin lesion, other\" code may apply for lesions with irregular borders. If the lesion has a pigmented pattern, you can add further specificity, such as \"L87.0 - Pigmentation, multiple, irregular\" or \"L87.1 - Pigmentation, multiple, irregular, large\". These codes aim to provide more detailed descriptions of the morphology, which may increase reimbursement coverage.\n\nWhen documenting the code, a physician should attempt to clearly describe the lesion's characteristics to reflect the complexity of the morphology, whether that includesAttempts issu",
      "source": "target",
